[[:db/retract 17592186307994 :answer-field/choices 17592186306258] [:db/retract 17592186308208 :answer-field/choices 17592186305972] [:db/retract 17592186307851 :answer-field/choices 17592186307331] [:db/retract 17592186307778 :answer-field/choices 17592186305669] [:db/retract 17592186308147 :answer-field/choices 17592186307032] [:db/retract 17592186308312 :answer-field/choices 17592186306472] [:db/retract 17592186307567 :answer-field/choices 17592186306688] {:db/id 17592186166919 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c87fc503-31b5-5d30-8770-67dd20112235" "question-9addc7f5-7ff7-5987-87ac-e05cc5e87102"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166545 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/73/737c96109e65d59f4df2c2fa574737fe604483fe4ccc9772a3d2bf22ee32e55a.svg)\nwhere ![](images/22/22dbda7d3b169206071e5af49fd1fe1af28d86ef1e0a8b37d58eacb62b44c1d2.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ![](images/5b/5bd691bbaa9542184d90e55aeb16f4dfcd3d22d092b4652e8bddba7bf0b926aa.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/8b/8b25789fb45234e4299e4b5ce3371c1afafc181c64f9802d212ae7cbefc0970f.svg)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](images/67/674212f0d815e0588faaf276bd797e9822ac5659e5d4b15a7635d7f1327d5dc5.svg) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/d4/d4c31285e6f31a229bc6fc5cbe2d78e2bf50f73bb6305d91687c5dcc31c3885a.svg)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](images/26/268764bbe776b03647daac7fc77e8ed24107e1bb0cf16d955f7a845a12c65755.svg)\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/4c/4c337f7f6b80f340859844e8217525db98230660d22e760c70218f0c282d21e9.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166920 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2b9f8e2-b39f-52a6-8433-15b3bb05fe5e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166546 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/72/72cfb513575d6481a4ee3206ff4d74568dc8397e10fec34ae89242a444a6b2d2.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/46/461a22d18752e55eada19b3f0bb58be61bee7a9cd88437304f291c4a9992bdae.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/ed/ed2fb464c555b2cbf0ffa7f67084e5565fc7c9e80e0263647e1303f69a91895d.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/52/523dccf73169d02b6bdf4297e96ec84bcc6c43213cdd722000f3a987a534b218.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](images/05/0589d1749aed66b91a94b49e4a8815bc4a8621eef3b45e535d2117e81d4f416d.svg)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6c84cd04f3b037bee48d02d3183114e2668fbbd110bd44e9311e7ff454db8bac.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6510dfcd-a99e-5730-8a08-a25b49094096" "question-fc362b82-7598-58e4-b9cc-cf1f2a033f38"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166547 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n![](images/dd/dda3aceadd2c3bc3b53422d9533f3b5926906de522137d2e0edc4794d658b1fd.svg)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32f9fd5ff34d75a7b82427a5ab7233613da3bdbaefdaef37058e65b1691b6c4a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c87fc503-31b5-5d30-8770-67dd20112235" :question/id #uuid "c87fc503-31b5-5d30-8770-67dd20112235" :question/math-academy-id "q-333291" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/bf/bffb2f815cc5a7db0c521054006aa834b17071f5dd33a97fe977e5d118b771be.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b2adb06a-393f-5309-9bba-b9aeaa0795a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b2adb06a-393f-5309-9bba-b9aeaa0795a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2adb06a-393f-5309-9bba-b9aeaa0795a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3b6b2dc2-3274-5ad6-936b-bec7b72f7ed1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f9a721ff-9da7-5833-a4f5-7626c00826c5" "answer-ee7f6321-f60f-5ec2-a00a-90e1ae1fddcf" "answer-3b6b2dc2-3274-5ad6-936b-bec7b72f7ed1" "answer-07399bf6-90a9-5db9-8898-b1137d5bdf22" "answer-6ad54cf4-a5bc-5525-be8a-085bcedf1ed5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9a721ff-9da7-5833-a4f5-7626c00826c5" :answer/id #uuid "f9a721ff-9da7-5833-a4f5-7626c00826c5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/14/14ae87fe329b09385dc0b4fa3238b6acfe428138bfdd438d99dd22cc12f94f9a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee7f6321-f60f-5ec2-a00a-90e1ae1fddcf" :answer/id #uuid "ee7f6321-f60f-5ec2-a00a-90e1ae1fddcf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b6b2dc2-3274-5ad6-936b-bec7b72f7ed1" :answer/id #uuid "3b6b2dc2-3274-5ad6-936b-bec7b72f7ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/bf/bffb2f815cc5a7db0c521054006aa834b17071f5dd33a97fe977e5d118b771be.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07399bf6-90a9-5db9-8898-b1137d5bdf22" :answer/id #uuid "07399bf6-90a9-5db9-8898-b1137d5bdf22" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ad54cf4-a5bc-5525-be8a-085bcedf1ed5" :answer/id #uuid "6ad54cf4-a5bc-5525-be8a-085bcedf1ed5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9addc7f5-7ff7-5987-87ac-e05cc5e87102" :question/id #uuid "9addc7f5-7ff7-5987-87ac-e05cc5e87102" :question/math-academy-id "q-333272" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ed34ebf7-d0c0-5560-bc6c-feaad1d7a4a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ed34ebf7-d0c0-5560-bc6c-feaad1d7a4a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed34ebf7-d0c0-5560-bc6c-feaad1d7a4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5c72915c-1617-5d86-9810-6ff702b7c1c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c72915c-1617-5d86-9810-6ff702b7c1c6" "answer-22875c88-e98a-556f-bd92-7742a4417f68" "answer-ba67a73d-dbf2-5baa-971e-eccb95c34b71" "answer-271019b1-15c6-5b35-bc59-a9d577948553" "answer-340a8062-814c-54fb-9be9-64f8d4dd51e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c72915c-1617-5d86-9810-6ff702b7c1c6" :answer/id #uuid "5c72915c-1617-5d86-9810-6ff702b7c1c6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22875c88-e98a-556f-bd92-7742a4417f68" :answer/id #uuid "22875c88-e98a-556f-bd92-7742a4417f68" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/74/745db6e4166ed6af3b7a3c1532bb23d7d6e7506f75c4f371430bd5d90a1a1d02.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba67a73d-dbf2-5baa-971e-eccb95c34b71" :answer/id #uuid "ba67a73d-dbf2-5baa-971e-eccb95c34b71" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-271019b1-15c6-5b35-bc59-a9d577948553" :answer/id #uuid "271019b1-15c6-5b35-bc59-a9d577948553" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/8e/8ebbd0147ad94cd62d952796b3171dba98b1388544fe5f1b379f981425477063.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-340a8062-814c-54fb-9be9-64f8d4dd51e6" :answer/id #uuid "340a8062-814c-54fb-9be9-64f8d4dd51e6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308423 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308424 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167237 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/2a/2acd3b0de16138b460a332cf6e4841e6caeeb5dbb23eef8f411dbdb5ce48ea42.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164485 :answer-field/correct 17592186164486 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308435 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/39/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | $0.22$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/39/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6dd454fbe71ba953b05c4da22d8c36f76277a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307994 :answer-field/correct 17592186307170 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308208 :answer-field/correct 17592186306701 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307851 :answer-field/correct 17592186306791 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307778 :answer-field/correct 17592186305976 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308147 :answer-field/correct 17592186306337 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2b9f8e2-b39f-52a6-8433-15b3bb05fe5e" :question/id #uuid "f2b9f8e2-b39f-52a6-8433-15b3bb05fe5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333485" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ y=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | $0.60$ |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | $3.58$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5cbe6e90-bfe2-50b2-9749-0734bd098c87" "answer-field-cf175a51-5f83-55f9-bec0-896057053ccd" "answer-field-2859a2b8-1b13-5844-925e-cb48d7716457" "answer-field-5907c15c-baff-518b-8fff-acdc90e66a25" "answer-field-bdb42f81-602d-565f-aa33-87e96fda9704" "answer-field-537d077f-ab4f-5655-a7a1-c6b68c8e2c8e" "answer-field-638c2f17-4597-5952-9fe9-dd882f69171d" "answer-field-7adbc8ed-eed3-5790-9d2d-6cc7f28de90b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5cbe6e90-bfe2-50b2-9749-0734bd098c87" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbe6e90-bfe2-50b2-9749-0734bd098c87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-37dbe076-2b24-564a-b897-6dbd753a26d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b080f209-a5d6-58c4-b3a7-f59017c49cc9" "answer-d463bf1f-816a-5b69-a640-ef6e4f5b0172" "answer-bc714fba-de8b-5e3b-97b8-199dc4128a6b" "answer-37dbe076-2b24-564a-b897-6dbd753a26d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b080f209-a5d6-